The '''Chūō Line''' ({{lang|ja|中央線}}, "Central Line") is a [[rapid transit]] line of the [[Okami Metro]] system in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. Designated as '''Line 4''', it is the fourth-oldest subway line in the city, having opened in 1967. The line is coloured '''green''' (#009E60) on system maps and route diagrams. It runs from {{station|Koi}} in the west to {{station|Nagatacho}} in the east, spanning a total of 26 stations over a distance of {{convert|36.5|km|abbr=on}}. The Chūō Line serves as the central east–west route through the heart of Okami, passing through major hubs including Okami Chuo, the National Diet of Okaiken, and Nagatacho.

As the primary east–west corridor of the Okami Metro, the Chūō Line traverses some of the city's most important political, commercial, and residential districts. The line passes through the wards of Karasaki, Mitsu-Minato, Sugamo, Chiyoda, Chuo, Tama, and Nagata, connecting the western suburbs to the governmental and business centre of the city. With 9 interchange stations, the Chūō Line provides critical connections to other metro lines, making it one of the most important and heavily used lines in the entire network.

Trains on the Chūō Line currently operate with 5-car formations using the [[#Rolling stock|7000 series]] Type D rolling stock, manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]] and introduced in 2025. The line is electrified at 1500&nbsp;V DC via [[overhead line]] and uses [[standard gauge]] (1435&nbsp;mm) track. The maximum operating speed is {{convert|80|km/h|abbr=on}}. The line is currently undergoing a fleet modernisation programme, transitioning from the older linear motor trains to the new 7000 series standard trains.

== History ==

The Chūō Line was opened in 1967 as the fourth subway line in the Okami Metro system. It was built to provide a central east–west route through the city centre, complementing the existing north–south lines and creating a more complete transit network for the rapidly growing metropolis. The name "Chūō" (中央, meaning "central") reflects the line's role as the primary cross-city corridor, running directly through the geographical and political heart of Okami. The original construction was a significant engineering undertaking, requiring deep tunnel boring through the dense urban core of the city.

The Chūō Line passes through several major hubs, including Okami Chuo, the National Diet of Okaiken, and Nagatacho. These stations serve some of the most important governmental and commercial destinations in the city. The National Diet of Okaiken - East station provides direct access to the national legislature, while Okami Chuo station serves as a major interchange point in the city's central business district. The line's route through these key areas has made it indispensable for both daily commuters and visitors to the capital.

Originally, the Chūō Line used 6-car trains. However, in the 1990s, the line was downsized to 5-car Type D trains when [[linear motor]] technology was introduced. The 3000 series linear motor trains, introduced in 1990, represented a significant technological shift for the line, allowing for smaller tunnel diameters and reduced construction costs for extensions. The linear motor technology was considered innovative at the time, and the 3000 series served the line for over three decades. However, as the technology aged and maintenance costs increased, plans were made to transition back to standard traction trains.

The line is now upgrading to the new 7000 series standard trains, introduced in 2025 and manufactured by Okami Heavy Industries. The 7000 series represents a return to conventional traction technology, offering improved reliability, ride comfort, and energy efficiency compared to the aging linear motor fleet. The 3000 series linear motor trains are being progressively retired. Additionally, the 4000 series flagship trains were introduced on both the Chūō and [[Miyoshi Line]]s in 2023, providing a premium passenger experience with advanced features and modern design. The 4000 series represents the cutting edge of Okami Metro's rolling stock, and its deployment on the Chūō Line underscores the line's importance in the network.

== Route description ==

The Chūō Line runs in a generally east–west direction across the width of Okami. From its western terminus at Koi in Karasaki Ward, the line travels east through Mitsu-Minato, Sugamo, and into the governmental heart of Chiyoda Ward. The line passes through the National Diet area before reaching Okami Chuo, the city's central business district. Continuing east, the line traverses Chuo Ward and Tama Ward before reaching its eastern terminus at Nagatacho in Nagata Ward.

The western section of the line, between Koi and Yamagata, serves primarily residential suburbs with a mix of coastal and hillside communities. The central section, between Inoko-machi and Komorebi, passes through the dense governmental and commercial core of the city, including the National Diet and Okami Chuo. The eastern section, from City Hall to Nagatacho, transitions through mixed commercial and residential areas before reaching the eastern terminus. The varied character of the districts along the route contributes to the line's high ridership and importance as a cross-city link.

